b816997c3e7eefcd45212e77dd81a6f885a5fdf" "q-95738/selection/bff16a7c95009ae5b57d3f464532cf50af7a0554274b8f04fab7eee6509cf789"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-95738" :question/id #uuid "a85f9c7b-82b5-5b0f-a187-ebfce33fc3b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-95738" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}.$ Differentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-95738/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b69474-34f6-5539-accf-5ebfc4f6eb0a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-95738"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-95693/selection/9783ac592299cb0884d8b9aa43d96013de8cace0406b45f4f78ef4c5eb1398af" :answer/id #uuid "0fcb7a3b-38d1-5ce4-9017-3b7a0c3f1de2" :answer/type :answer