803adc23b6e1647626063ccb0" "q-95866/selection/b4ef2cf548b18fdad83421e50124fb1da2f8fbf1cbcf6474a7f884c116008e9a" "q-95866/selection/5bf16c0465091293e2ccf7996a58e462c5676e3ff66044d55996b77a0fc2c78c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-95866" :question/id #uuid "12a5c105-0a97-59bc-b4b6-006716adb140" :question/math-academy-id "q-95866" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2}{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{2}{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|\\frac{x}{2}|<1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-95866/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5aabe1c9-7a37-5b34-893f-9338f5c7c69a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-95866"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-95858/selection/13227f96d2f0d554c5de8a9b51c817e302faf08f21340573f33f0266a368a336" :answer/id #uuid "3eb13989-b580-5ceb-9b34-952783341e86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95858/selection/73f90dd12f29292432654783fcb0e5d65d0ec1aa23b408a06061a02c32d357f9" :answer/id #uuid "c1e083c5-ad15-5b43-b462-87a5df5db6b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95858/selection/d5a30a6a2218b201875f556645fac75334aa4918f60e2fe01299794e4de8a02e" :answer/id #uuid "ad6018b4-68f5-5ed9-97eb-8226cb26ac66" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12\\operatorname{artanh}⁡4x+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95858/selection/c78cb4688d55ad49f32e0ff85872b2f32405e0299bb9176430856c8e87e94b03" :answer/id #uuid "16aad334-daaf-5153-9d31-97f7a98baa77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\operatorname{artanh}⁡4x+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95858/selection/f9a61c78c64f49434d11f69cf01e54f10c6befdad81d23db0f76b7c418135582" :answer/id #uuid "a2ea3755-6328-5188-aa27-cd07e9b833aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡4x+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95858/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "08d2dcf8-dc2b-5a59-a779-289e4186a4de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-95858/selection/c78cb4688d55ad49f32e0ff85872b2f32405e0299bb9176430856c8e87e94b03" :answer-field/choices ["q-95858/selection/13227f96d2f0d554c5de8a9b51c817e302faf08f21340573f33f0266a368a336" "q-95858/selection/73f90dd12f29292432654783fcb0e5d65d0ec1aa23b408a06061a02c32d357f9" "q-95858/selection/d5a30a6a2218b201875f556645fac75334aa4918f60e2fe01299794e4de8a02e" "q-95858/selection/c78cb4688d55ad49f32e0ff85872b2f32405e0299bb9176430856c8e87e94b03" "q-95858/selection/f9a61c78c64f49434d11f69cf01e54f10c6befdad81d23db0f76b7c418135582"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-95858" :question/id #uuid "1e8e55ec-821d-58af-9dc6-39f20d66e28f" :question/math-academy-id "q-95858" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{12}{1-16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $16{x}^{2}=(4x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=4x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{12}{1-16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =12\\int \\frac{1}{1-(4x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{artanh}⁡4x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|4x|<1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-95858/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5aabe1c9-7a37-5b34-893f-9338f5c7c69a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-95858"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-95900/selection/e3f8c9286f82206e27503336889af52483b6887d4175ba9847de8ed0b7bfc9ee" :answer/id #uuid "44aefa4d-4618-548d-9153-75c833f17991" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{11}}{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95900/selection/0ece251b252f82914657b3b64d60e93a8e66a6105d2382277cf3269c37071ac8" :answer/id #uuid "8138c0c3-616d-5658-b990-19605f10eed4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+11}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95900/selection/caaac23563c43434a4ebf6977a4dcba85e928890027d00738732b86c895e0ab6" :answer/id #uuid "c950bb2e-560a-50d3-9817-15b83bc9d20e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{11}}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95900/selection/9a1567283c6b716152d571ec3e1c2555cb899824f84a3db5a9e02f4d7ed8f02c" :answer/id #uuid "6a0d136a-059b-5695-919f-7ff2916d45cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{11}}{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{2}})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95900/selection/2554af720bb5958a907fa1e1fffdb0d6db6de151788bf6c7fb9e33f69c60b197" :answer/id #uuid "55ac6216-a96c-5343-8301-cc21053a58bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{11}}{2\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{2}})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95900/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "470f8280-d808-502c-9782-4fcc20a4c1fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-95900/selection/9a1567283c6b716152d571ec3e1c2555cb899824f84a3db5a9e02f4d7ed8f02c" :answer-field/choices ["q-95900/selection/e3f8c9286f82206e27503336889af52483b6887d4175ba9847de8ed0b7bfc9ee" "q-95900/selection/0ece251b252f82914657b3b64d60e93a8e66a6105d2382277cf3269c37071ac8" "q-95900/selection/caaac23563c43434a4ebf6977a4dcba85e928890027d00738732b86c895e0ab6" "q-95900/selection/9a1567283c6b716152d571ec3e1c2555cb899824f84a3db5a9e02f4d7ed8f02c" "q-95900/selection/2554af720bb5958a907fa1e1fffdb0d6db6de151788bf6c7fb9e33f69c60b197"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-95900" :question/id #uuid "b76a3529-18ed-5306-b58f-30d89bf12248" :question/math-academy-id "q-95900" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3}}\\,\\text{d}x=3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse hyperbolic cosine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $3$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $2\\sqrt{3}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}}^{2\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}}^{2\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡u{|}_{\\sqrt{3}}^{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =9(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡2\\sqrt{3}-\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =9(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{{(2\\sqrt{3})}^{2}-1})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}-1})) \\\\  & =9(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{11})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{2})) \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{11}}{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{2}})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-95900/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd13fafc-d765-5e44-aa5a-79c2fff71212"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-95900"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-346519/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "4b9106a2-a41c-5286-925e-b27c3bdbc8d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-346519/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "cecfdc2c-0da0-5a50-a0b8-92dc7f045bce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-346519/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-346519/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-346519/field-2/8de7a84a41666d4612460439d17949f75cc9ba4883ff75d5034a1786c2a06792" :answer/id #uuid "7c08e2de-7356-5e0e-aae9-74476960a431" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-346519/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "5cf46f78-25b4-5dc1-ae89-06193e4318c4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-346519/field-2/8de7a84a41666d4612460439d17949f75cc9ba4883ff75d5034a1786c2a06792" :answer-field/choices ["q-346519/field-2/8de7a84a41666d4612460439d17949f75cc9ba4883ff75d5034a1786c2a06792"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-346519" :question/id #uuid "4834efb9-b435-5d43-9063-edbca8c7e7ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-346519" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{20}{25-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$artanh\\,(${{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nNote that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{20}{25-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C,\n$$\n\nthe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{5}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=5\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{20}{25-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{3/5}\\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{3/5}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{3/5} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})-4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡0 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-346519/field-1" "q-346519/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd13fafc-d765-5e44-aa5a-79c2fff71212"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-346519"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-350220/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "51de2cad-8271-5428-82b1-e4217bff8f6f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-350220/field-1/695792c47319cb1ed098a6d83038b7ad6d032838f9103d693bf346f1dc234ff8" :answer/id #uuid "d6bdd88d-8bcd-57b6-8c7a-1ed153c9aa4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-350220/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "31faa681-e4b4-53fb-80d8-39d3018ae65b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-350220/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-350220/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-350220/field-1/695792c47319cb1ed098a6d83038b7ad6d032838f9103d693bf346f1dc234ff8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-350220/field-2/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" :answer/id #uuid "445853b9-3323-5389-b5d6-37e44ea5324e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-350220/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "89331699-d334-5016-958d-0b3ef7054104" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-350220/field-2/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" :answer-field/choices ["q-350220/field-2/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-350220" :question/id #uuid "0802b7c5-9d48-5121-89e7-6efe6d0dee84" :question/math-academy-id "q-350220" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{40}{64-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}\n$artanh\\,(${{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nNote that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{40}{64-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{5}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C,\n$$\n\nthe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{8})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{8}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{8}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=8\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{40}{64-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{3/4}\\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 8\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{3/4}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{3/4} \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})-5\\operatorname{artanh}⁡0 \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-350220/field-1" "q-350220/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd13fafc-d765-5e44-aa5a-79c2fff71212"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-350220"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-96968/selection/a5d720e4e08eec78b96de7f0c37b90794d558e4d50b9d73a4dd9cf718415a20f" :answer/id #uuid "84810f5f-bec8-5489-b25b-8cc1ab7b4340" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\sqrt{6})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96968/selection/f08310408bb83eede890982e98bcad40cebf199d01c5ed5a692879aa64ef75af" :answer/id #uuid "4bb7b8ad-0b1a-5170-adec-ddfeceaa7ec8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96968/selection/da487432916a53d24c04940ab81f6138c9a682887bafc82488bc441ab744b5dd" :answer/id #uuid "b04a35ab-9efe-5627-ba2e-8b3584673ee8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+\\sqrt{6})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96968/selection/a7eace2aa76f92fe38870ad957460a33288f198bd1fa4a864620d4b7937fbdd1" :answer/id #uuid "47e03339-bcfc-5551-b012-e0bf849db2fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96968/selection/47d0dabf43c3f863174e67ce05e5d684e79ae39ae4fbee18dde88ad267956457" :answer/id #uuid "af760edd-6aa7-586b-99fc-58da2500efe0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6+\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96968/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "75c7f5cf-d06e-5906-8467-fe75c4149778" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-96968/selection/f08310408bb83eede890982e98bcad40cebf199d01c5ed5a692879aa64ef75af" :answer-field/choices ["q-96968/selection/a5d720e4e08eec78b96de7f0c37b90794d558e4d50b9d73a4dd9cf718415a20f" "q-96968/selection/f08310408bb83eede890982e98bcad40cebf199d01c5ed5a692879aa64ef75af" "q-96968/selection/da487432916a53d24c04940ab81f6138c9a682887bafc82488bc441ab744b5dd" "q-96968/selection/a7eace2aa76f92fe38870ad957460a33288f198bd1fa4a864620d4b7937fbdd1" "q-96968/selection/47d0dabf43c3f863174e67ce05e5d684e79ae39ae4fbee18dde88ad267956457"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-96968" :question/id #uuid "5b3f91e3-21ba-5093-99c3-24c8174787f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-96968" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+36}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+36}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse hyperbolic sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{6}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{6}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=6\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+36}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\cdot (6\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡1-\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+\\sqrt{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-96968/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd13fafc-d765-5e44-aa5a-79c2fff71212"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-96968"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-95899/selection/5583514fc7466755eee753873860ebadeafaacb7557c57bd2d5047cb227886d0" :answer/id #uuid "5888cbbf-5764-541d-8aeb-53a61dd72a87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\sqrt{5})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95899/selection/00bea3c16bbd305fe3166d8043d2e113f130e82546bf5125c9f13a427ec4f387" :answer/id #uuid "d295de2a-f151-50d6-9c3c-d1d8cc95e2f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5+\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95899/selection/1bbc89f049b1bedbdab6778d17db0228d5bfc12969aea322996ed6b9fe4a1f48" :answer/id #uuid "26403460-332c-53ce-b62d-a6604876997b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95899/selection/a9a4ac7fb6b68806d1c1dea99259b9bfcf91d724c3cf27c6a1197236ea181315" :answer/id #uuid "7ff2d026-ff65-5507-ae35-5cbad9f96d5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3+\\sqrt{5})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95899/selection/d41be3fc21548c7396aef941046bf061ba9823a67a11e1add52e8b19dfef743d" :answer/id #uuid "2d905c41-9b7e-5b57-95fe-2b2d0a6d890a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5+\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95899/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e07e4bd8-efdc-5500-8eb2-b0e64edd79f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-95899/selection/5583514fc7466755eee753873860ebadeafaacb7557c57bd2d5047cb227886d0" :answer-field/choices ["q-95899/selection/5583514fc7466755eee753873860ebadeafaacb7557c57bd2d5047cb227886d0" "q-95899/selection/00bea3c16bbd305fe3166d8043d2e113f130e82546bf5125c9f13a427ec4f387" "q-95899/selection/1bbc89f049b1bedbdab6778d17db0228d5bfc12969aea322996ed6b9fe4a1f48" "q-95899/selection/a9a4ac7fb6b68806d1c1dea99259b9bfcf91d724c3cf27c6a1197236ea181315" "q-95899/selection/d41be3fc21548c7396aef941046bf061ba9823a67a11e1add52e8b19dfef743d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-95899" :question/id #uuid "d152ec8f-5780-567c-ae16-c5ae13de3aed" :question/math-academy-id "q-95899" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}}\\,\\text{d}x=4{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse hyperbolic sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\cdot (2\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡2-8\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡0 \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡2 \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\sqrt{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-95899/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd13fafc-d765-5e44-aa5a-79c2fff71212"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-95899"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8393"] :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{\\pi}{\\sqrt{2x^2+1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^2+1}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = \\arsinh u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $2x^2 = \\left(\\sqrt{2}\\,x\\right)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u =\\sqrt{2}\\,x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\sqrt{2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{2}\\,x)}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{\\arsinh}u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{\\arsinh}(\\sqrt{2}\\,x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8394"] :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{x^2-4}} \\,\\textrm{d}x   =$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nLet's rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{x^2-4}}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{3}{2} \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^2-1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic cosine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^2-1}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = \\arcosh u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u = \\dfrac x 2.$ Differentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= \\dfrac{1}{2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x =2\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =3\\text{\\arcosh}u+C \\\\  & =3\\text{\\arcosh}(\\frac{x}{2})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8395"] :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{10}{1 - 4x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x   =$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-u^2} \\, \\textrm{d}u =\\artanh u +C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $4x^2 = \\left(2x\\right)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2\\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{10}{1-4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\int \\frac{1}{1-(2x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =5\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =5\\text{\\artanh}u+C \\\\  & =5\\text{\\artanh}2x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $\\left|2x \\right| <    1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8396"] :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nEvaluate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2 - 4}}\\, \\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nNote that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2 - 4}}\\, \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac {x}{2} \\right)^2 - 1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse hyperbolic cosine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^2-1}}\\, \\textrm{d}u = \\arcosh{u}+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\dfrac {x}{2} \\right)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u = \\dfrac {x}{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = 2\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $4$ | $2\\sqrt{5}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $\\sqrt{5}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2 - 4}}\\, \\textrm{d}x &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac {x}{2} \\right)^2 - 1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\[5pt]\n\n&= \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{2}^{\\sqrt 5} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{u^2 - 1}} \\cdot (2 \\, \\textrm{d}u) \\[5pt]\n\n&= \\int_{2}^{\\sqrt 5} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\sqrt{u^2 - 1}} \\[5pt]\n\n&= \\arcosh u \\Big|_{2}^{\\sqrt 5} \\[5pt]\n\n&= \\arcosh{\\sqrt 5} - \\arcosh{2} \\[5pt]\n\n&=  \\ln\\left(\\sqrt 5 + \\sqrt{\\left(\\sqrt 5\\right)^2 - 1}\\right) - \\ln\\left(2 + \\sqrt{\\left(2\\right)^2 - 1}\\right)  \\[5pt]\n\n&= \\ln\\left(2 + \\sqrt{5}\\right) -  \\ln\\left(2 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)  \\[5pt]\n\n& = \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{2 + \\sqrt{5}}{2 + \\sqrt{3}}  \\right)\n\n}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
